    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now tick claims and open them all with one button. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec, including the \"bulk open\" test, which checks the numbering order and the audit lines.\n\n**How it behaves**\n- Each claim row has a checkbox. Below the quick-add form there's an \"Open selected claims\" button.\n- The selected claims are opened one at a time, in list order: people in the order they were added, and each person's claims newest first. Each one is opened exactly as the single \"Open\" button would open it: it takes the next claim number and is refused for the same reasons, such as being dated in a closed period or not being a draft.\n- A claim that can't be opened is left as it is, and nothing about it is written to the store or the audit file. The ones that do open write the usual `CLAIM_OPENED`, `ROLLUP` and `TOTALS` audit lines, in the same order as opening them by hand.\n- Afterwards the screen shows how many were opened and how many were refused, and the selection is cleared. A selected claim that's no longer on the sheet counts as refused.\n\n**Where the changes are**\n- **Server:** a new `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/claims/open` endpoint that takes a list of claim ids. The new method, `ClaimService.openAll`, gives each claim its own transaction, so one refusal doesn't undo the others.\n- **Front end:** the checkbox is in `claims.ts`. The selection, the button and the counts are in `people.ts`, and `claims-api.ts` has a new `openAll` call.\n\nI also added a `bulk-open-error` message, which the test doesn't use. It shows if you press the button with nothing selected or if the request fails, so the screen never just does nothing. There was no database change.",
